WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.200
In this video, we'll go over logistic regression.

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:10.600
This is a learning algorithm that you use when the output labels Y in a supervised learning problem are all either 0 or 1,

00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:13.000
so for binary classification problems.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:23.000
Given an input feature vector X may be corresponding to an image that you want to recognize as either a cat picture or not a cat picture,

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:31.000
you want an algorithm that can output a prediction which you only call Y hat, which is your estimate of Y.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:40.000
More formally, you want Y hat to be the probability of the chance that Y is equal to 1 given the input feature X.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:49.000
So in other words, if X is a picture, as we saw in the last video, you want Y hat to tell you what is the chance that this is a cat picture.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:01:03.000
So X, as we said in the previous video, is an X dimensional vector, given that the parameters of logistic regression will be W,

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:11.000
which is also an X dimensional vector, together with B, which is just a real number.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:20.000
So given an input X and the parameters W and B, how do we generate the output Y hat?

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:27.000
Well, one thing you could try that doesn't work would be to have Y hat be W transpose X plus B,

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:32.000
you know, kind of a linear function of the input X.

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:37.000
And in fact, this is what you use if you were doing linear regression.

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:45.000
But this isn't a very good algorithm for binary classification because you want Y hat to be the chance that Y is equal to 1.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:50.000
So Y hat should really be between 0 and 1.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:58.000
And it's difficult to enforce that because W transpose X plus B can be much bigger than 1 or can even be negative,

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:03.000
which doesn't make sense for a probability that you want to be between 0 and 1.

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:12.000
So in logistic regression, our output is instead going to be Y hat equals the sigmoid function applied to this quantity.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:14.000
This is what the sigmoid function looks like.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:23.000
If on the horizontal axis I plot Z, then the function sigmoid of Z looks like this.

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:27.000
So it goes smoothly from 0 up to 1.

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:30.000
Let me label my axes here.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:34.000
This is 0. And it crosses the vertical axis at 0.5.

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:38.000
So this is what sigmoid of Z looks like.

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:43.000
And we're going to use Z to denote this quantity W transpose X plus B.

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:46.000
Here's the formula for the sigmoid function.

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:52.000
Sigmoid of Z, where Z is a real number, is 1 over 1 plus e to the negative Z.

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:54.000
So notice a couple of things.

00:02:54.000 --> 00:03:01.000
If Z is very large, then e to the negative Z will be close to 0.

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:07.000
So then sigmoid of Z will be approximately 1 over 1 plus something very close to 0

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:11.000
because e to the negative of a very large number will be close to 0.

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:13.000
So this is close to 1.

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:20.000
And indeed, if you look on the plot on the left, if Z is very large, then sigmoid of Z is very close to 1.

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:27.000
Conversely, if Z is very small or if it is a very large negative number,

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:39.550
then sigmoid of Z becomes 1 over 1 plus e to the negative Z.

00:03:39.550 --> 00:03:42.550
And this becomes e to the huge number.

00:03:42.550 --> 00:03:48.550
So this becomes, you know, think of it as 1 over 1 plus a number that is very, very big.

00:03:48.550 --> 00:03:56.450
And so that's close to 0.

00:03:56.450 --> 00:04:00.450
And indeed, you see that as Z becomes a very large negative number,

00:04:00.450 --> 00:04:03.450
sigmoid of Z goes very close to 0.

00:04:03.450 --> 00:04:09.450
So when you implement logistic regression, your job is to try to learn parameters W and B

00:04:09.450 --> 00:04:15.450
so that Y hat becomes a good estimate of the chance of Y being equal to 1.

00:04:15.450 --> 00:04:18.450
Before moving on, just another note on the notation.

00:04:18.450 --> 00:04:26.450
When we program neural networks, we'll usually keep the parameter W and the parameter B separate,

00:04:26.450 --> 00:04:29.450
where here B corresponds to an interceptor.

00:04:29.450 --> 00:04:34.450
In some of the classes, you might have seen a notation that handles this differently.

00:04:34.450 --> 00:04:41.450
In some conventions, you define an extra feature called X0, and with that equal to 1.

00:04:41.450 --> 00:04:53.450
So then now X is in R of nX plus 1, and then you define Y hat to be equal to sigma of theta transpose X.

00:04:53.450 --> 00:05:02.450
In this alternative notational convention, you have a vector parameter theta, theta 0, theta 1, theta 2,

00:05:02.450 --> 00:05:06.450
down to theta nX.

00:05:08.780 --> 00:05:17.780
And so theta 0 plays a row of B, that's just a row number, and theta 1 down to theta nX plays a row of W.

00:05:17.780 --> 00:05:25.780
It turns out when you implement your neural network, it would be easier to just keep B and W as separate parameters.

00:05:25.780 --> 00:05:31.780
And so in this class, we will not use any of this notational convention that I just wrote in red.

00:05:31.780 --> 00:05:35.780
If you've not seen this notation before in other classes, don't worry about it.

00:05:35.780 --> 00:05:43.780
It's just that for those of you that have seen this notation, I wanted to mention explicitly that we're not using that notation in this course.

00:05:43.780 --> 00:05:47.780
But if you've not seen this before, it's not important and you don't need to worry about it.

00:05:47.780 --> 00:05:51.780
So you've now seen what the logistic regression model looks like.

00:05:51.780 --> 00:05:56.780
Next, to train the parameters W and B, you need to define a cost function.

00:05:56.780 --> 00:05:58.780
Let's do that in the next video.
